Risk and Incident Response Plan

Scenario: Insider Threat Response at a University

1. Purpose

The purpose of this plan is to outline the actions the university will take to identify, contain, respond to, and recover from unauthorized access to the payroll system. This plan also identifies the risks associated with the incident and the long-term preventive measures needed to strengthen the university’s security posture.

2. Scope

This plan applies to:

The Finance Department

University payroll systems

Internal staff accounts

University IT & Security teams

Any third-party systems connected to payroll or HR data

3. Risk Assessment
3.1 Threat Overview

The incident involves suspicious late-night logins to the payroll system from an internal staff account. Potential threat actors include:

A malicious or careless employee

A curious student accessing staff credentials

An external attacker using stolen credentials

A social engineering or phishing attack

3.2 Vulnerabilities Identified

The investigation uncovered several weaknesses:

Weak or reused passwords

Lack of multi-factor authentication (MFA)

Passwords written on sticky notes and left unsecured

Little to no monitoring of after-hours access

No automated alerts for suspicious network activity

Lack of staff cybersecurity training

3.3 Risk Impact
CIA Triad	Impact	Description
Confidentiality	High	SSNs, bank accounts, and salary data could be exposed.
Integrity	Medium	Payroll data could be altered or manipulated.
Availability	Medium	System downtime during investigation disrupts finance operations.

Overall Risk Level: High

4. Incident Response Plan

The response plan follows the NIST Incident Response Lifecycle:
Preparation → Detection → Containment → Eradication → Recovery → Lessons Learned

4.1 Detection & Analysis

System logs flagged late-night access from an unrecognized device

IT Security confirmed the staff member was not online during those hours

Multiple failed login attempts were observed prior to the successful login

Network traffic analysis performed to check for IP masking or Tor usage

Decision: Classify the event as a probable unauthorized access incident.

4.2 Containment

Immediate actions taken:

Temporarily locked the affected staff account

Forced university-wide password resets for finance staff

Disabled remote access for the account until secure authentication was enabled

Logged and preserved all access data for forensic review

Blocked suspicious IP addresses and devices from further attempts

4.3 Eradication

Steps taken to remove threat components and prevent recurrence:

Removed all unauthorized sessions

Scanned endpoints for malware or credential-stealing tools

Eliminated outdated credentials and password lists found in desks

Implemented automated monitoring rules for unusual after-hours access

Reviewed permission levels for all finance department accounts

4.4 Recovery

Actions to bring systems back to normal:

Restored full access to payroll systems after verifying they were uncompromised

Enabled MFA for all payroll users prior to reactivation

Reconfigured log settings to increase visibility into suspicious behavior

Conducted a controlled re-opening of payroll access with new security requirements

Verified all financial data for integrity and accuracy

4.5 Lessons Learned

The organization must strengthen credential protection and remove poor practices such as password reuse and unsecured physical notes.

MFA must be required for all sensitive university systems.

Regular cybersecurity training is necessary to combat phishing and social engineering.

Automated security alerts must be deployed to detect possible insider or external threats earlier.

The university must implement continuous monitoring and auditing procedures.

5. Preventive Controls and Recommendations
5.1 Access Control Improvements

Enforce least-privilege access

Enable MFA for all finance, HR, and admin accounts

Require complex passwords (minimum 12 characters, no reuse)

Prohibit sticky notes, shared passwords, and insecure storage of credentials

Conduct quarterly user account audits

5.2 Network and System Controls

Deploy Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) monitoring

Enable alerts for after-hours access and unusual device logins

Implement IDS/IPS to detect abnormal traffic patterns

Require endpoint protection on all staff devices

5.3 Training & Awareness

Mandatory training every semester on:

Phishing

Password hygiene

Reporting suspicious activity

Distribute quick-reference guides for secure login practices

5.4 Policy Updates

Update Access Control Policy

Update Password Policy

Implement After-Hours Access Logging Policy

Enforce disciplinary action for policy violations

6. Communication Plan Summary

As part of the response:

IT will notify leadership and finance managers within 24 hours of any high-risk incident

Staff must report unusual login alerts immediately

Communication channels include email, ticketing system, and secure messaging

Monthly cybersecurity bulletins will keep staff informed of updates and reminders

7. Conclusion

This incident demonstrates how weak credentials and lack of multi-factor authentication can create serious vulnerabilities, even within trusted university environments. By implementing the recommended controls—especially MFA, password hygiene enforcement, continuous monitoring, and staff training—the university will significantly reduce its exposure to insider threats and unauthorized access attempts.

This response plan provides a structured approach to managing the current incident and preventing future breaches across the university’s financial systems.
